            <Abstract>
                <AbstractText Label="CONTEXT" NlmCategory="BACKGROUND">- Invasive pancreatic ductal adenocarcinoma has a greater than 90% mortality rate at 5 years. Understanding noninvasive, curable precursor lesions gives us the best hope for reducing mortality from pancreatic ductal adenocarcinoma. The 3 pancreatic precursor lesions that have been well studied include intraductal papillary mucinous neoplasm, mucinous cystic neoplasm, and pancreatic intraepithelial neoplasia.</AbstractText>
                <AbstractText Label="OBJECTIVE" NlmCategory="OBJECTIVE">- To give an update on the latest clinical, molecular, and pathologic advances in intraductal papillary mucinous neoplasm, mucinous cystic neoplasm, and pancreatic intraepithelial neoplasia for the general surgical pathologist.</AbstractText>
                <AbstractText Label="DATA SOURCES" NlmCategory="METHODS">- The current literature was analyzed and the authors' experiences with institutional and consult material were incorporated.</AbstractText>
                <AbstractText Label="CONCLUSIONS" NlmCategory="CONCLUSIONS">- Our understanding of the molecular alterations that lead from pancreatic precursor lesion to invasive carcinoma continues to evolve. These advances aid clinicians in their treatment decisions and researchers in their search for actionable, druggable targets.</AbstractText>
            </Abstract>
